WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.199
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.460
SPEAKER: In the last video,
we talked about the developer

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.700
experience and how to enhance
the developer experience based

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.720
on understanding their needs.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.630
Let's take it to the
next step and understand

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.060
how to attract and
engage developers.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.710
To get started,
let's take a look

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.450
at the digital value chain.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.310
Most customers who
are just embarking

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.080
on their digital
journey will start

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.750
on the right side of the
chain and simply expose

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.550
the back-end data assets.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.620
The developers will
appreciate getting access

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.540
to the back-end
data until they have

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.310
to manipulate
large payloads that

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:36.890
affect the performance
of their application.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.950
That's why it's so important
to know your target audience.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.050
Moving from right to
left, the API team

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.380
who's charged with exposing the
back-end data for developers

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.500
needs to understand the
application developer's needs.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.440
Using the customer journey
and personas technique

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.770
to frame up a sample use
case and taking the returns

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:58.080
material authorization
process as an example,

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.620
the API team will need to
determine how much data

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.810
is needed to process the RMA.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.390
If the only data needed
is the last few orders,

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.610
don't send the
entire order history.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.920
Likewise, if only
customer name and address

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.690
is needed for the
RMA, then don't

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.280
send extraneous customer data
that can slow down performance.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.990
The digital value chain helps
identify your target audience.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.510
Most people can relate
to the end customer who

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.030
uses the mobile apps to
order coffee, purchase

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.370
products, or track deliveries.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.010
And most people overlook
the application developer

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.720
as a target audience.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.300
Depending on where you
reside on the digital value

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.390
chain will depend on who
your target audience is.

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.730
Looking right to left,
the target audience

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.240
for the back-end
developer is the API team.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.750
The target audience for the
developer is the customer.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.170
Now take a look at the middle
of the digital value chain.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.060
The target audience
for the API team

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.870
is the developer who
consumes the API product.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.660
Now let's take a closer
look at the developer.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.080
The application developer is
also known as the API consumer.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.940
The developer community
is very diverse.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.520
We often hear from
companies that

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.250
believe a developer
community is restricted only

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.780
to internal developers.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.180
However, the developer
community includes

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.600
internal developers, partner
developers, and developers

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.640
external to the company.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.640
Successful communities include
all types of developers

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.300
and even goes so far as to
treat both internal and external

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.830
developers the same.

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.860
Companies with a focused
API product strategy

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.510
will look outwards
to engage developers.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.630
So if your target
audience for API products

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.810
is your developer community,
you will need to actively engage

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.190
this community.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:42.700
There are three key areas of
focus in developer engagement.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.420
One, understand the needs
of your API consumers,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.370
what do they need
to discover APIs,

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.010
and understand them in
order to get started.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.500
Number two, market
your API product

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.350
like you would market any
other product or service.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.680
Just publishing to a developer
storefront is not enough.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.980
You want to drive developers
to your storefront.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.210
Number three, adoption is key
to the success of your program.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.700
You will need to define the
metrics for adoption in order

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.340
to track and measure
your progress.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.010
Is the number of developers
registered on your developer

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.170
portal the best
measure of success?

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.660
Or perhaps it's the number
of API products used

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.580
in production applications.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.380
The storefront for
developer engagement

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.380
has a number of parallel
paths to be thought through.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.000
Acquisition of
developers is key.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.610
The developer portal
needs to catch

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.920
the attention of the
developer community,

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.660
and the developer
must know how to get

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.640
started with the API products.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.600
How do you go about doing this?

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.840
We will talk about
it in the next slide.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.640
The developer portal
is a storefront

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:43.930
for your API products.

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.970
Therefore, the
product catalog needs

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.100
to be compelling to developers.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.710
Documentation must be
complete and comprehensive

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.270
for quick adoption.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.270
Ensure that the onboarding
process is frictionless.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:57.040
The easier you make
it for the developer

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.690
to register and
obtain a key so they

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.490
can get started with
your application

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.950
is key to fast adoption.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:06.520
And finally, don't forget about
ongoing maintenance and support

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:07.630
of your storefront.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:10.720
And more importantly,
your API consumers.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.090
If developers have questions on
the use of your API products,

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:17.440
be sure to provide a
forum to get answers.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.290
Let's dig into a few
of these concepts.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:24.220
Follow the 5-5-5 rule for
developer acquisition.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.220
That is, you want to grab their
attention in the first five

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:30.850
seconds, build interest
in the next five seconds,

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.010
build a case in the
next five minutes.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.740
Grab their attention by making
your storefront visually

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.730
stunning.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.910
Create an API catalog with
thorough documentation,

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:43.480
making it easy to understand.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.700
Ensure that you have
a value proposition

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:49.270
by making a compelling
case to use your APIs.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:52.210
Create a Getting Started page
providing a step-by-step guide

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.220
on how to work with your APIs.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.620
Showcase your customer
success stories.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.560
This can spark ideas
for future innovation.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.120
Make it easy to get started.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.910
Don't hide documentation
behind registration.

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.130
Make them want to register.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:10.330
Finally, get them engaged by
hosting events like hackathons.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.190
Proper documentation
will facilitate

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.440
onboarding and adoption.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.870
Putting the effort into
complete documentation

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:18.880
up front will improve
the adoption rate

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.460
and down the road can reduce
the amount of support needed.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.550
Note that documentation needs
to be maintained over time,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.610
so plan on a team
to update content

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.410
on your developer portal.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:31.570
Make your onboarding
process frictionless

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:33.340
by minimizing the
amount of information

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.960
required to register.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.330
We recommend obtaining
basic information needed

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.110
to identify the developer,
such as name, email address,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:44.620
company, and perhaps a brief
description of the application

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.720
the developer plans on creating.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.610
The easier you make
the registration,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.160
the faster you can
onboard the developer.

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.760
Self-service on your developer
portal is key to adoption.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.440
What does this mean?

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.020
You want to engage
developers by making API

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.420
products discoverable to them.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.360
This doesn't mean you are giving
them the keys to the kingdom.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.450
You can grab their attention by
associating your API products

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.060
with a brief description
of what they can do.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.550
You have the ability to
prove API key requests,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.580
giving developers access
to your API products.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.630
In addition, permissions
based on the type of developer

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:21.880
can help you tailor the
storefront experience

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.300
for internal, external,
or partner developers.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.480
Lastly, provide the
capability for developers

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.090
to try out the API
products on the spot

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.850
will go a long way
towards faster adoption.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:35.960
A few years ago, I was
working with a customer who

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:37.990
was preparing for
a demonstration

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.030
to allow a patient to
update their prescription

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.350
on a mobile app.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.300
The application
developer was represented

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.840
by a firm external to my
customer and based offshore.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.380
Of course, in these situations,
there's always a time crunch.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.990
The application developer
working with my customer

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:56.140
had less than a week to complete
the demo application in time

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.780
to showcase it at the
annual HIMSS conference.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.050
Yes, we were all under the
gun to create the application,

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:04.780
get access to the API
product, and showcase

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.760
the self-service
features of the Google

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.280
[INAUDIBLE] developer portal.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:11.020
The first step was
for the developer

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.980
to register on the developer
portal and request an API key.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:17.170
The access to the API
product was granted

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.300
in the space of a couple hours.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:21.310
Then it was all about
engaging the developer

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.140
through complete
documentation so he

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.370
knew how to work with the API.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.670
Once the developer
had access to the API,

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.950
his app development was quick.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:32.380
Fast forward to the HIMSS
conference for that year,

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.300
and my customer was
able to showcase

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.880
a cool mobile app allowing
patients to update

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.220
their current prescriptions.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.130
My customer had done this
by exposing an API product

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:45.580
giving access to patient
electronic health records.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:47.950
Like all well-managed
API products,

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.010
this one required an API
key in order for developers

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.810
to access the API.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.420
The process of
granting or declining

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:58.480
the request for an API key is
available through the developer

00:07:58.480 --> 00:07:59.910
portal.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:04.020
The developer remarked on the
speed of granting the API key.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.000
He mentioned that
in the past, it

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.800
had taken two weeks
from the time he

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:11.490
submitted the request to the
acquisition of the API key.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.400
Acquiring the API key within
two hours of the request

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.160
was phenomenal and greatly
speeded up the time

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.360
to deploy the mobile app.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:21.360
Once your storefront
is up and running,

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.490
don't forget to put effort
into support and maintenance.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:27.240
Keep your documentation
fresh and up to date.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.580
Provide online forums,
blogs, and frequently

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.159
asked questions to reduce the
effort required to support

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.480
the developer portal.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.630
Treat your online storefront
like a retail channel

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.049
and spend the resources to
keep it a vibrant community.

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.429
Your API consumers
are important.

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:44.790
Focus on addressing
your API consumer

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:48.360
needs with an API First
approach and make engaging

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.620
with your company effortless.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:53.640
I will leave you with a
few best practice tips.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.010
Make your API
catalog discoverable,

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:59.670
make registration on your
developer portal frictionless,

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:03.240
ensure that your content
is engaging and up to date,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.180
and finally, give your
developers the ability

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.710
to engage with you by enabling
them to test their application.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.840
This will ensure quality
rollout for everyone.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.460
I hope you found the topic of
developer engagement engaging.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.010
Be sure to check out the next
video on adoption strategies.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.910
Thanks for watching.

